1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:08,498
RxDataLint adds a review step before turning medicines data into a chart. This
walkthrough uses the bundled synthetic example, not patient or

2
00:00:08,498 --> 00:00:13,747
company records. Open a CSV, understand the findings, and export evidence before
analysing totals.

3
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:23,609
Extract the Windows ZIP and open Start Checker. Click Try sample to load eight
fictional records. For public SCMD data, use Choose CSV.

4
00:00:23,609 --> 00:00:28,127
Processing stays on your computer, and the desktop remains responsive during
checks.

5
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:37,199
The sample produces six errors and nine warnings, but only four affected records.
One row can trigger several rules, so fifteen findings do not

6
00:00:37,199 --> 00:00:41,462
mean fifteen bad rows. These counts are not a regulatory or clinical verdict.

7
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:50,578
Choose Negative to focus on quantities and costs below zero. A stock adjustment
can legitimately be negative. Source values stay intact: the

8
00:00:50,578 --> 00:00:56,337
program does not delete them, turn them positive, or decide they are wrong. Review
their context.

9
00:00:57,540 --> 00:01:05,296
Select a finding and double click, or press Enter, to reach its full explanation.
Details link the finding to its medicine, organisation, month

10
00:01:05,296 --> 00:01:10,692
and original value. You have a specific record to investigate, instead of a vague
dashboard number.

11
00:01:11,890 --> 00:01:20,113
Check coverage shows which rules ran, which were skipped, and why. Executed does
not mean passed. No findings does not mean every possible

12
00:01:20,113 --> 00:01:23,688
quality issue was tested. Read the coverage and guidance together.

13
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:32,858
Search an organisation, medicine, month, or rule while using a category filter.
Here, R two B leaves six negative findings. Category totals

14
00:01:32,858 --> 00:01:39,377
describe the unsearched file; the visible count describes your current view. Clear
the search to widen your review.

15
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:49,377
Export full report keeps all findings even when the screen is filtered. It creates
HTML, JSON evidence, and a normalized CSV when the schema

16
00:01:49,377 --> 00:01:55,241
permits. Export filtered findings creates a smaller follow up list. Normalization
does not correct flagged records.

17
00:01:56,440 --> 00:02:04,938
Open Start Analysis for SQL summaries. Use the sample with source synthetic, or
provide the official URL for public data. Generate and verify

18
00:02:04,938 --> 00:02:09,372
builds SQLite summaries and bilingual offline reports, then checks the exported
files.

19
00:02:10,570 --> 00:02:19,004
Blank costs stay unknown; malformed costs are separate problems. The missing cost
card counts blanks only, so zero percent missing does not mean

20
00:02:19,004 --> 00:02:24,137
every cost is valid. Excluding negatives changes totals. That difference is not a
saving.

21
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:33,899
Switch language, choose a theme, and adjust font size. These controls change
presentation, not data or findings. Scroll on smaller screens and

22
00:02:33,899 --> 00:02:38,957
open details for long fields. The analysis launcher has the same display controls.

23
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:48,064
RxDataLint reviews medicines data. BatchScope is a separate synthetic
manufacturing quality project, with its own repository and Windows release.

24
00:02:48,064 --> 00:02:56,385
Both are educational alpha tools. This demo combines real application captures,
explanatory animation, and a synthetic English voice. Start with

25
00:02:56,385 --> 00:02:58,881
the example and review the evidence.

